{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:28891792 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:28891792 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"28891792","source":"MED","pmid":"28891792","pmcid":"PMC5614561","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC5614561"]},"doi":"10.7554/elife.26952","title":"PGAM5 promotes lasting FoxO activation after developmental mitochondrial stress and extends lifespan in <i>Drosophila</i>.","authorString":"Borch Jensen M, Qi Y, Riley R, Rabkina L, Jasper H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Borch Jensen M","lastName":"Borch Jensen","firstName":"Martin","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8875-0345"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Buck Institute for Research on Aging, Novato, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Qi Y","lastName":"Qi","firstName":"Yanyan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Buck Institute for Research on Aging, Novato, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Riley R","lastName":"Riley","firstName":"Rebeccah","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Buck Institute for Research on Aging, Novato, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Rabkina L","lastName":"Rabkina","firstName":"Liya","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Buck Institute for Research on Aging, Novato, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Jasper H","lastName":"Jasper","firstName":"Heinrich","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6014-4343"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Buck Institute for Research on Aging, Novato, United States."},{"affiliation":"Immunology Discovery, Genentech, South San Francisco, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6014-4343"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8875-0345"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"6","journalIssueId":2591457,"dateOfPublication":"2017 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2017,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Elife","title":"eLife","essn":"2050-084X","issn":"2050-084X","isoabbreviation":"Elife","nlmid":"101579614"},"printPublicationDate":"2017-09-01"},"pubYear":"2017","pageInfo":"e26952","abstractText":"The mitochondrial unfolded protein response (UPR<sup>mt</sup>) has been associated with long lifespan across metazoans. In <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i>, mild developmental mitochondrial stress activates UPR<sup>mt</sup> reporters and extends lifespan. We show that similar developmental stress is necessary and sufficient to extend <i>Drosophila</i> lifespan, and identify Phosphoglycerate Mutase 5 (PGAM5) as a mediator of this response. Developmental mitochondrial stress leads to activation of FoxO, via Apoptosis Signal-regulating Kinase 1 (ASK1) and Jun-N-terminal Kinase (JNK). This activation persists into adulthood and induces a select set of chaperones, many of which have been implicated in lifespan extension in flies. Persistent FoxO activation can be reversed by a high-protein diet in adulthood, through mTORC1 and GCN-2 activity. Accordingly, the observed lifespan extension is prevented on a high-protein diet and in FoxO-null flies. The diet-sensitivity of this pathway has important implications for interventions that seek to engage the UPR<sup>mt</sup> to improve metabolic health and longevity.","affiliation":"Buck Institute for Research on Aging, Novato, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institute on Aging","grantId":"R01 AG028127","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute on 